# Repository-root ignores.
#
# Added by Production MS4. `backend/.gitignore` and `frontend/.gitignore` already covered their own
# trees, but nothing covered the root — so a `.env` written next to `docker-compose.yml` (which is
# exactly where its header tells you to put one) was tracked by default. Nothing of the sort has
# ever been committed; this makes that a property of the repository rather than of everyone's care.

### Env — real values, never committed. `.env.example` / `.env.production.example` are the ###
### placeholder-only templates and are deliberately NOT ignored.                           ###
.env
.env.*
!.env.example
!.env.production.example

### Local-disk fake storage (pronto.storage.mode=local) at the repository root ###
/data/

### QA scratch files (manual smoke-test payloads/output, not fixtures) ###
qa-tmp/
qa-tmp-*/

### Terraform (Production MS5, infra/terraform/) ###
# Downloaded provider plugins -- ~700 MB per module, restored by `terraform init`.
**/.terraform/*
# State. The real state lives in the S3 backend (infra/terraform/backend.tf); anything matching
# these locally is either the bootstrap module's state or a stray local run, and both describe real
# infrastructure. `.tfstate.backup` too, which is the same content one apply behind.
*.tfstate
*.tfstate.*
# Saved plan files are binary and contain every resolved value in the plan.
*.tfplan
# Human-readable plan output (`terraform plan > somefile.out`). Same content, in text, so it is the
# easier of the two to leak: it carries every resolved endpoint, ARN and account id in the plan.
# Added after `bootstrap-plan.out` turned out to be matched by no existing rule.
*plan.out
# Real variable values, and the backend config that names an AWS account id. The committed
# templates are terraform.tfvars.example (deliberately un-ignored below) and the backend.hcl shape
# documented in backend.tf.
terraform.tfvars
*.auto.tfvars
backend.hcl
!terraform.tfvars.example
# .terraform.lock.hcl is deliberately NOT ignored -- it pins provider versions and checksums, and
# committing it is what makes an apply reproducible.

### OS / editor ###
.DS_Store

# Generated one-shot QA task definitions. They embed a BCrypt password verifier for the QA account,
# which must never be published -- a hash is offline-crackable in a way an ARN is not. The SQL and
# the README beside them are the reviewable artefacts; these JSONs are rebuilt on demand.
infra/bootstrap/pronto-qa-*.taskdef.json
